<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cielito Lindo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">popularized <a href="/years/1882">1882</a>; its first verse from a Spanish song of the early 17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Cielito Lindo&quot; (roughly &quot;Lovely Sweet One&quot;) is a Mexican folk song, or copla, popularized in 1882 by the Mexican author Quirino Mendoza y Cortés (c. 1862–1957). It is widely known by words from its refrain, &quot;Canta y no llores&quot;, or as the &quot;Ay, Ay, Ay, Ay song&quot;. Its verses follow the seguidilla, a classical Castilian stanza of seven lines of alternating seven and five syllables, and singers add and drop verses freely.</p></div>
